# Implement tables and ruled educational blocks

## Scope and existing evidence

The implementation notes below retain their original scope and status.
The closing decision is a bounded composite design: only merged table
structure, Rule and Label are currently admitted semantic capabilities.

Support merged cells, headers, alignment, wrapping, ruler-like borders, boxes,
dividers, arrows, labels, and page references.

Current executable evidence makes table header rows semantically distinct from
ordinary body rows without coupling that meaning to visual styling. Candidate
acceptance preserves the row role and stable row identity together with nested
cell content. An exact-base accepted edit can change only one row role and keeps
row identity and cells unchanged, creates one revision only for a real change,
and treats no-op, stale, absent, non-row, and empty-session requests as typed
no-effects. Nested tables remain addressable through callouts, list items, and
table cells across successive revisions.

Merged cells now carry nonzero logical row and column spans. The first row fixes
logical table width, later rows fill unoccupied columns around inherited row
spans, and candidate acceptance rejects horizontal conflicts, vertical overflow,
or rows that leave logical columns uncovered before accepted identity
allocation. A 20,000-case occupancy oracle now matches the validator's exact
first typed failure and semantic owner, not only valid/invalid classification.

An exhaustive 9,724-case compact corpus additionally covers zero through three
rows, zero through two cells per row, and every one- or two-row/column span
combination. Accepted promotion preserves valid spans exactly, and validation
keeps logical coverage compact instead of allocating per spanned column.

The occupancy generator also asserts every row/cell-count bucket, every 1–3
row/column span, valid grids, and all three typed grid failure classes are
actually reached.

A read-only logical-placement projection now exposes each validated cell's exact
semantic row/cell identity, zero-based logical row/column start, and existing
span in semantic row order. It reuses the validator's grid walk and returns no
partial placement set for an invalid table. These are logical topology indices,
not physical layout, wrapping, alignment, border, or renderer coordinates.
Its placement output is also checked against the independent occupancy model for
all 20,000 generated table cases and the complete 9,724 compact-grid corpus,
including typed invalid-grid parity rather than valid layouts alone.

The same validated topology now exposes its compact logical column count only
after complete-grid validation. Generated and exhaustive occupancy oracles check
that count with identical invalid-grid failure precedence, including the full
nonzero `u32` cell-span range without per-column allocation.

Wide tables with thousands of interleaved inherited row spans now skip earlier
active intervals by binary search rather than scanning every inherited cell for
every placement. The first possible overlap remains the only overlap candidate,
preserving the existing column-span and row-span rejection precedence. A new
8,192-pair interleaved-grid fixture and an adjacent inherited-span fixture
exercise the compact walk; generated and exhaustive occupancy oracles continue
to check exact placements, typed errors, and validated topology behavior.

A separate 4,096-column, 512-row fixture now keeps all inherited row spans
active across hundreds of later rows. The table walk preserves the sorted
interval invariant through expiry and skips sorting entirely on rows that add
no spans; it still sorts after new spans are appended, preserving the same
next-row overlap and cursor behavior.

A constructor-sealed logical-topology view now binds one exact table to its
validated column count and complete caller-order cell placements. The borrowed
evidence cannot survive mutation of that table, and generated plus exhaustive
occupancy oracles require it to preserve the same first structural failure as
the standalone validator. The sealed view can also resolve the exact cell whose
logical span covers one row/column index; both table corpora cross-check every
in-range logical slot plus out-of-range misses. It adds no physical coordinates,
wrapping, alignment, border, measurement, hit-testing, or rendering policy.

An exact-base direct cell-span edit reuses that complete table-grid invariant. A
valid edit preserves cell identity and child blocks, creates one accepted
revision, and enters Undo history; a structurally invalid replacement is a typed
no-effect. Table-cell spans are now Structured-content editable values in the
generic simulator. Ordered span commands validate cloned owning-table candidate
grids, Apply revalidates affected final tables atomically, and one Undo restores
the complete batch.

A zero-content `Rule` block already supplies the semantic identity for a
divider.
Candidate acceptance preserves that block kind and its generic block style
reference, so divider meaning is not flattened into paragraph text or inferred
from drawn geometry. Ruler-like divider measurement and rendering remain open.

Label text now has its own semantic block kind and editable inline-span
authority, so diagram labels need not be flattened into figure pixels or generic
paragraphs. Diagram topology, arrows, page references, label attachment, cell
alignment and wrapping, ruler-like border geometry, table measurement/layout,
and command-batch cell content operations remain open.

## Finite educational structure and edit-identity contract

**Inputs:** one accepted notebook revision, two-page semantic authority,
unique accepted table/row/cell/label/divider identities, declared cell spans,
and a separate set of proposed educational drawing and reference relations.
**Outputs:** an admitted merged logical table, semantic blocks where supported,
or an explicit `Unresolved(Unsupported)` owner for unsupported presentation
and linking semantics. No visual coordinates or cross-page link targets can
be invented from inline source text.

**One representative classroom example:**

- **Merged header table:** table `T1` has header row `R1` containing cell
  `C1` spanning two columns and one row, with editable span "Experiment".
  Body row `R2` contains distinct cells `C2` ("Mass") and `C3` ("Time"),
  each spanning one column and one row. The valid two-column topology has
  no gaps or overlapping inherited spans. `T1`, both rows, all cells,
  and their inline spans retain distinct semantic identities.
- **Ruled box:** `B1` is a semantic `Callout` with child paragraph "Key fact"
  and a distinct style identity `S1`. The proposed box-outline meaning
  remains presentation metadata requiring an admitted style/renderer;
  assigning `S1` alone does not claim an actual border was drawn.
- **Divider:** `D1` is a native `Rule` block with its own stable block ID.
- **Arrow:** `A1` describes a directed relationship from `B1` to `T1`.
  Since no `Arrow` semantic kind or admitted endpoint schema exists, retain
  the exact authored relationship in an `Unresolved(Unsupported)` block
  with stable owner `A1`; do not fake diagram geometry.
- **Label:** `L1` is a native `Label` block whose editable inline span `L2`
  contains "Input". The label's attachment to an arrow is not inferred.
- **Page reference:** `PRef1` proposes a link to accepted page `P2` with
  visible text "see next page". No cross-page reference schema or navigation
  authority exists yet, so preserve this as another explicit unresolved
  source block instead of a functional link.

**Finite editing rules:** changing the header row role or admitted cell span
requires exact current revision and complete grid validation, then creates
one new revision with unchanged table/cell identity. Stable Rule and Label
identities survive unrelated semantic edits and Undo/Redo under new revision
identities. Future arrow and page-link admission must validate endpoint kinds,
page existence and change effects before replacing unresolved source.

**Refusal:** a header `C1` spanning three columns in this fixed two-column
table, a zero span, a body row with an uncovered logical column, or a cycle
in a future reference schema must not be silently accepted. A stale row edit
cannot mutate the current accepted table. Source text that visually resembles
an arrow or page number does not grant drawing or navigation authority.

**Assumptions and residual risks:** the implemented grid validator owns
logical topology and current row/span edits. Box borders, alignment, table
text measurement, wrapped cells, arrow endpoints, cross-page navigation,
physical ruler drawing and rendered styling have no full first-release
implementation. The two unresolved examples are deliberate safety fallbacks,
not functional arrow or page-reference objects.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical for the six-part educational composition,
  2026-11-02; practical evidence applies only to merged table topology,
  editable row/span semantics, Rule and Label block identities.
- **Delivered:** six named educational examples with stable owner IDs,
  explicit source-retaining fallbacks, valid two-column logical topology,
  edit/refusal rules and integration limitations.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** visual ruled boxes, arrows,
  functional page references, cell wrapping/alignment, layout and renderer
  consumption. None is counted as a working capability merely because its
  design or unsupported placeholder has been recorded.
